from math12_reviewed_unit1 import heading,paragraph,page,figure
from math12_reviewed_higher_derivatives import note
from math12_native_geometry import geometry,path
def graph(letter,m,c,xticks,yticks):
 xmin,xmax,ymin,ymax=-8,6,-6,8
 def pt(x,y):return (65+(x-xmin)*28,270-(y-ymin)*17)
 bounds=sorted([xmin,xmax,(ymin-c)/m,(ymax-c)/m]);lo,hi=bounds[1:3]
 paths=[path([pt(xmin,0),pt(xmax,0)]),path([pt(0,ymin),pt(0,ymax)]),path([pt(lo,m*lo+c),pt(hi,m*hi+c)],stroke='#be123c')]
 labels=[(*pt(xmax+.2,0),'x'),(*pt(0,ymax+.5),'y')]
 for x in xticks:
  v=pt(x,0);paths.append(path([(v[0],v[1]-4),(v[0],v[1]+4)]));labels.append((v[0]-10,v[1]-10,str(x)))
 for y in yticks:
  v=pt(0,y);paths.append(path([(v[0]-4,v[1]),(v[0]+4,v[1])]));labels.append((v[0]-23,v[1]+5,str(y)))
 return geometry('Exercise 7.2 Question 1('+letter+')','Coordinate line with source intercepts '+letter,paths,labels)
GRAPHS=[graph('a',1,5,[-5],[5]),graph('b',-3,6,[2],[6]),graph('c',-.75,-3,[-4],[-3])]
QUESTIONS=[
'Find the equation of lines that are represented on the coordinate planes: (a), (b), (c), as shown in the source graphs.',
'In each case, find the slope, if it is defined: (a) (−5,4) and (3,6); (b) 4x+7y = 1; (c) Parallel to 2y−4x = 7; (d) Perpendicular to 6x = y−3.',
'What are the x- and y-intercepts for each of the following lines? (a) y = 2x+6; (b) y = −3x+9; (c) y = x+2.',
'In each case, show that the pair of lines are parallel or perpendicular or neither: (a) x−2y−6 = 0, 2x+y−5 = 0; (b) 3x+4y−8 = 0, x+3y−2 = 0; (c) 2x−y−7 = 0, 4x−2y−5 = 0; (d) 2x−5y−7 = 0, 6x−15y+5 = 0.',
'In each case, find the equation of a line that passes through the pair of points: (a) O(0,0) and A(2,6); (b) E(1,0) and F(2,5); (c) I(1,1) and J(3,3).',
'In each case, find the equation of a line that passes through the point A(x₁,y₁) having slope m: (a) A(1,2), m = 4; (b) A(−1,−2), m = −1/2; (c) A(−3,5), m = −3; (d) A(7,−8), m = 5.',
'In each case, find the equation of a line that exists the y-intercept c and slope m: (a) c = 2, m = 2; (b) c = 4, m = 8; (c) c = −4, m = 1/2.',
'Transform the equation 7x−10y+13 = 0 into: (a) slope intercept form; (b) symmetric form; (c) normal form.']
STEPS=[
['Read the signed intercepts from each source graph; each line has two different crossing points.','(a) The crossings are (−5,0) and (0,5), so the slope is (5−0)/(0−(−5)) = 1.','Thus (a) is y = x+5, or x−y+5 = 0.','(b) The crossings are (2,0) and (0,6), so the slope is (6−0)/(0−2) = −3.','Thus (b) is y = −3x+6, or 3x+y−6 = 0.','(c) The crossings are (−4,0) and (0,−3), so the slope is (−3−0)/(0−(−4)) = −3/4.','Thus (c) is y = −3x/4−3, or 3x+4y+12 = 0. Substitute each pair of crossings to check its equation.'],
['For a nonvertical line, use the coordinate difference quotient or isolate y.','(a) m = (6−4)/(3−(−5)) = 2/8 = 1/4.','(b) 4x+7y = 1 becomes y = −4x/7+1/7, so m = −4/7.','(c) 2y−4x = 7 becomes y = 2x+7/2, whose slope is 2.','A parallel line has the same slope, so (c) m = 2.','(d) 6x = y−3 becomes y = 6x+3, whose slope is 6.','A perpendicular line has slope −1/6 because 6(−1/6) = −1. All four requested slopes are defined.'],
['For an x-intercept set y = 0; for a y-intercept set x = 0.','(a) 0 = 2x+6 gives x = −3; at x = 0, y = 6.','The crossing points for (a) are (−3,0) and (0,6).','(b) 0 = −3x+9 gives x = 3; at x = 0, y = 9.','The crossing points for (b) are (3,0) and (0,9).','(c) 0 = x+2 gives x = −2; at x = 0, y = 2.','The crossing points for (c) are (−2,0) and (0,2). These are signed intercepts, not unsigned distances.'],
['Isolate y in both equations of each pair. Equal finite slopes mean the same direction; product −1 means perpendicular.','(a) y = x/2−3 and y = −2x+5 have slopes 1/2 and −2. Their product is −1, so they are perpendicular.','(b) y = −3x/4+2 and y = −x/3+2/3 have slopes −3/4 and −1/3.','The slopes in (b) differ and their product is 1/4, so they are neither parallel nor perpendicular.','(c) y = 2x−7 and y = 2x−5/2 have equal slopes 2 and different intercepts; they are parallel distinct lines.','(d) y = 2x/5−7/5 and y = 2x/5+1/3 have equal slopes 2/5 and different intercepts; they are parallel distinct lines.','These classifications use the exact printed constants, including the +5 in the final equation.'],
['Compute the slope for each pair, then use one endpoint in y−y₁ = m(x−x₁).','(a) m = (6−0)/(2−0) = 3, so the line through the origin is y = 3x.','It passes through A because 6 = 3·2.','(b) m = (5−0)/(2−1) = 5; using E gives y−0 = 5(x−1).','Thus (b) y = 5x−5; F satisfies 5 = 10−5.','(c) m = (3−1)/(3−1) = 1; using I gives y−1 = x−1.','Thus (c) y = x, and both I and J satisfy it.'],
['Use the point-slope form y−y₁ = m(x−x₁).','(a) y−2 = 4(x−1), so y = 4x−2.','(b) y+2 = −(x+1)/2, so y = −x/2−5/2, or x+2y+5 = 0.','(c) y−5 = −3(x+3), so y = −3x−4.','(d) y+8 = 5(x−7), so y = 5x−43.','Check (a) at x = 1 gives 2, (b) at x = −1 gives −2, (c) at x = −3 gives 5, and (d) at x = 7 gives −8.','The coefficients of x in these solved equations are the four prescribed slopes.'],
['The slope-intercept form is y = mx+c.','(a) Substitute m = 2 and c = 2: y = 2x+2.','(b) Substitute m = 8 and c = 4: y = 8x+4.','(c) Substitute m = 1/2 and c = −4: y = x/2−4.','At x = 0 these give y = 2,4,−4, respectively, confirming each signed intercept.','A unit increase in x changes y by 2,8,1/2, respectively, confirming the slopes.'],
['(a) Isolate y: 10y = 7x+13, hence y = (7/10)x+13/10.','(b) A point on the line is (1,2), because 7−20+13 = 0.','A direction vector is (10,7), perpendicular to the coefficient normal (7,−10).','Normalize the direction by √149: cosθ = 10/√149, sinθ = 7/√149.','A symmetric form is (x−1)/(10/√149) = (y−2)/(7/√149) = r. Equivalently (x−1)/10 = (y−2)/7 uses a rescaled parameter.','(c) The unit normal must point from the origin toward the nearest point, giving (−7/√149,10/√149).','Divide the equation by −√149 to obtain −7x/√149+10y/√149 = 13/√149.','Its positive right side p = 13/√149 is the perpendicular distance; the normal angle satisfies cosφ = −7/√149 and sinφ = 10/√149.','Check the nearest point (−91/149,130/149): it satisfies 7x−10y+13 = 0 and has distance 13/√149.']]
ANSWERS=['(a) x−y+5 = 0; (b) 3x+y−6 = 0; (c) 3x+4y+12 = 0.','(a) 1/4; (b) −4/7; (c) 2; (d) −1/6.','(a) −3,6; (b) 3,9; (c) −2,2 (x-intercept, y-intercept).','(a) perpendicular; (b) neither; (c) parallel; (d) parallel.','(a) y = 3x; (b) y = 5x−5; (c) y = x.','(a) y = 4x−2; (b) x+2y+5 = 0; (c) y = −3x−4; (d) y = 5x−43.','(a) y = 2x+2; (b) y = 8x+4; (c) y = x/2−4.','(a) y = (7/10)x+13/10; (b) (x−1)/(10/√149) = (y−2)/(7/√149); (c) −7x/√149+10y/√149 = 13/√149.']
PROJECT={'question':'How can you calculate the midpoint between your home and school/college? Calculate this distance and write the procedure.','steps':['Choose a Cartesian map with consistent distance units and mark home H(x₁,y₁) and school S(x₂,y₂).','The midpoint of their straight connecting segment is M = ((x₁+x₂)/2,(y₁+y₂)/2).','The straight-line distance is d = √[(x₂−x₁)²+(y₂−y₁)²].','For a checked sample only, take H(0,0) and S(6,8) km; the textbook supplies no numerical coordinates.','Then M = (3,4) km and d = √(36+64) = 10 km.','Both distances HM and MS are √(9+16) = 5 km, confirming the midpoint.','Actual road distance may differ from straight-line distance. For a midpoint along a chosen road route, measure the route and locate its halfway point; it need not be the coordinate average. Use your own map values for a personal response.'],'answer':'Use the coordinate-average midpoint and Euclidean straight-line distance; actual values depend on the home/school locations.','sourceQuestionBlocks':[figure('unit7-home-school-project','Project — home and school/college','Original illustration of a home and school/college connected by a distance line')]}
